# Code owners for OneLiteFeatherNET/Falco.
#
# Two rules of this file are easy to get wrong, so they are spelled out here:
#
#  1. GitHub silently drops any owner that lacks write access to this
#     repository. A rule naming a non-existent team or a read-only collaborator
#     looks correct in review and does nothing. Every handle below is a current
#     admin of this repository.
#  2. Unlike .gitignore, the LAST matching rule wins. Order matters.
#
# Why handles and not a team: the sister repositories own their code through a
# per-repository maintainers team (Cyano uses @OneLiteFeatherNET/cyano-maintainers,
# Aves uses @OneLiteFeatherNET/aves-maintainers). Falco has no equivalent —
# `falco-maintainers` does not exist in the organisation, and no team at all is
# granted access to this repository, so a team entry would be ignored without a
# warning. Once a `falco-maintainers` team exists and holds write access, it
# should replace the handles below and this file falls back in line with the
# rest of the organisation.

# Default owners for everything, including the Java modules falco-anvil,
# falco-light, falco-instance and falco-benchmarks, and the documentation under
# docs/.
*                               @TheMeinerLP @theEvilReaper

# Release, publishing and CI surface.
#
# These paths decide what leaves the repository rather than what it contains: the
# root build script holds the maven-publish setup, the repo.onelitefeather.dev
# endpoints and the single version line Release Please rewrites; settings.gradle.kts
# holds the credentialed resolution repository and the version catalogue; the
# wrapper is a binary executed by every CI run; and the workflows are thin callers
# of the shared OneLiteFeatherNET/workflows jobs that run with `secrets: inherit`.
# A mistake here ships a broken or mis-versioned artefact instead of failing a
# build, which is why they are pinned explicitly and not left to the default line
# alone. The owners are the same as above today; the point is that widening the
# default to a broader team later must not silently widen who can approve a
# release.
/build.gradle.kts               @TheMeinerLP @theEvilReaper
/settings.gradle.kts            @TheMeinerLP @theEvilReaper
/gradle/wrapper/                @TheMeinerLP @theEvilReaper
/release-please-config.json     @TheMeinerLP @theEvilReaper
/.release-please-manifest.json  @TheMeinerLP @theEvilReaper
/CHANGELOG.md                   @TheMeinerLP @theEvilReaper
/.github/                       @TheMeinerLP @theEvilReaper
